The final sample included 41,288 adults (Table 1
                     ). The overall 24-h movement composition was associated with CmRS (p < 0.001); greater relative sleep and MVPA were associated with lower CmRS, whereas sedentary behavior was associated with higher CmRS (Table 2
                     ). Reallocating time toward sleep or MVPA was associated with lower CmRS and toward sedentary behavior with higher CmRS (Fig. 2
                     ; Supplementary Table S3). For 30-min reallocations, changes were −0.0060 (95% CI −0.0077 to −0.0043) for sleep, +0.0069 (0.0058 to 0.0080) for sedentary behavior, and −0.0221 (−0.0264 to −0.0178) for MVPA; other activities were not significant.
